[THOUGHT] The patient is unable or unwilling to provide occupational, footwear, or alcohol history — this gap limits assessment of mechanical or toxic contributors. Since further probing may not yield new data and we must stay within 16 turns, I’ll proceed to domain #7: targeted physical examination. Given the unilateral, positionally triggered sole tingling, the most relevant exam elements are:  
- Sensory testing (light touch, pinprick) over the medial/lateral plantar nerves (S1/S2 distribution) to map the affected dermatome;  
- Tinel’s sign at the tarsal tunnel (medial ankle, posterior to medial malleolus) to assess for nerve irritation;  
- Straight leg raise (SLR) test to evaluate for lumbar radicular tension (though foot sole involvement suggests S1, and SLR is more sensitive for L4–S1 radiculopathy);  
- Foot posture and arch assessment (pes planus increases tarsal tunnel risk);  
- Vascular exam (dorsalis pedis/posterior tibial pulses, capillary refill) given smoking history.  

Of these, Tinel’s sign at the tarsal tunnel is the most specific initial maneuver for focal entrapment — and it’s non-invasive, rapid, and highly informative.

[TEST] Perform Tinel’s sign at the tarsal tunnel: tap gently over the posterior tibial nerve just behind and inferior to the medial malleolus, and ask if it reproduces the tingling sensation in the sole of the right foot.

